[{:db/id -1, :working/body "# MTP (Gemma 4) Speculative-Decoding Bench — jacob (M4 16 GB) PoC\n\n**Date**: 2026-05-13\n**Host**: jacob (Apple M4, 16 GiB unified, macOS 26.3.1)\n**Scope**: 1-node PoC. No fleet rollout, no `serve_plain.py` integration.\n**Reference**: <https://blog.google/innovation-and-ai/technology/developers-tools/multi-token-prediction-gemma-4/>\n\n## TL;DR\n\n| Config | mean tok/s | speedup | acc/round | drafter efficiency |\n|---|---:|---:|---:|---:|\n| baseline (no drafter) | 20.85 | 1.00× | — | — |\n| **mtp_b3** | **23.05** | **1.11×** | 0.34 | 11.4% |\n| mtp_b4 | 20.14 | 0.97× | 0.42 | 10.5% |\n| mtp_b6 | 15.42 | 0.74× | 0.50 | 8.4% |\n| mtp_b8 | 12.34 | 0.59× | 0.52 | 6.5% |\n\n**Verdict**: On this single-stream M4 16 GB box, the *OptiQ-4bit target + bf16 MTP drafter* pair gives a **1.11× best case at block_size=3** — well below the **~2.2×** Google reports for batch 4–8 on Apple Silicon. Larger block sizes monotonically *hurt* throughput because acceptance saturates around 0.5–0.8 tokens/round while every rejected draft costs a full target verification step.\n\nDrafter never exceeds ~0.83 accepted tokens/round even on its best prompts (`code_py`, `reasoning`), suggesting that either (a) the 4-bit target's logits diverge enough from the bf16 drafter's prediction to bound acceptance, or (b) batch=1 throughput is already memory-bandwidth-bound — adding speculative work doesn't pay off at the bottom of the batch curve. Most likely both.\n\n## Setup\n\n| | |\n|---|---|\n| **Target model** | `mlx-community/gemma-4-e4b-it-OptiQ-4bit` (6.1 GB, text-only quant) |\n| **Drafter** | `mlx-community/gemma-4-E4B-it-assistant-bf16` (182 MB, 78.8M params, 4 layers) |\n| **Drafter kind** | `mtp` (Gemma 4 native MTP head — last-layer hidden + per-layer-type shared K/V) |\n| **Runtime** | mlx 0.31.2, mlx-vlm 0.5.0, mlx-metal 0.31.2, transformers 5.8.0, Python 3.13.12 |\n| **Method** | T=0 (greedy → byte-identical to baseline), `MAX_TOKENS=128`, 1 warmup per config |\n| **Prompts** | 5 prompts (short_qa, code_py, code_explain, creative_short, reasoning) |\n| **Harness** | `/tmp/mtp-bench/bench.py` (custom; mlx-vlm CLI doesn't expose acceptance counters) |\n| **Raw results** | `/tmp/mtp-bench/results-full.json` |\n\nThe OptiQ-4bit checkpoint strips audio/vision towers, but `mlx_vlm.utils.load` instantiates the full multimodal class. Worked around with a one-line monkey-patch defaulting `nn.Module.load_weights(strict=False)` before importing `mlx_vlm`. Generated text is unaffected (text path only).\n\n## Per-prompt throughput (tok/s)\n\n| prompt | gen tokens | baseline | mtp_b3 | mtp_b4 | mtp_b6 | mtp_b8 |\n|---|---:|---:|---:|---:|---:|---:|\n| short_qa | 2 | 10.28 | 12.53 | 11.61 | 9.78 | 8.75 |\n| code_py | 128 | 24.20 | **27.52** | 25.01 | 19.12 | 14.79 |\n| code_explain | 61 | 24.11 | **25.78** | 22.12 | 15.14 | 12.28 |\n| creative_short | 20 | 21.98 | **23.27** | 19.51 | 14.16 | 11.01 |\n| reasoning | 128 | 23.67 | **26.16** | 22.46 | 18.92 | 14.87 |\n\n`short_qa` produces only 2 tokens (chat template emits an immediate stop after the assistant header) — not a meaningful workload but kept in for completeness.\n\n## Per-prompt acceptance (tokens/round)\n\n| prompt | mtp_b3 | mtp_b4 | mtp_b6 | mtp_b8 |\n|---|---:|---:|---:|---:|\n| short_qa | 0.000 | 0.000 | 0.000 | 0.000 |\n| code_py | 0.411 | 0.641 | 0.803 | 0.803 |\n| code_explain | 0.419 | 0.512 | 0.476 | 0.550 |\n| creative_short | 0.429 | 0.429 | 0.429 | 0.429 |\n| reasoning | 0.443 | 0.524 | 0.803 | 0.829 |\n\nAcceptance scales sub-linearly with block_size (`creative_short` is flat — drafter exhausts its predictive horizon at 1–2 tokens). Even at b=8, drafter efficiency tops out at 6–10%.\n\n## Memory\n\nPeak GPU memory was **8.78 GB across every config** — the bf16 drafter (~158 MB resident) is negligible against the 6.1 GB 4-bit target plus KV cache. No OOM risk on 16 GB unified.\n\n## Why so far below Google's claim?\n\nGoogle's ~2.2× number is for **batch 4–8 on bf16 target**. We tested **batch 1 on a 4-bit target**, which is the worst case for speculative decoding for two compounding reasons:\n\n1. **Batch 1 = bandwidth-bound**, not compute-bound. The target is already running near its memory-bandwidth ceiling, so the \"verify N drafts in one matmul\" win is small. Speculative decoding shines at higher batch where the matmul has compute headroom to absorb the extra width.\n2. **4-bit target ≠ bf16 drafter**. Quantization noise widens the logit gap between target and drafter, lowering acceptance. The drafter was trained against the bf16 target.\n\nExpected next step (out of scope per user decision): rerun on **bf16 target** at **batch 4** via `mlx-vlm` server mode, which is where Google measured.\n\n## Observations / gotchas for future fleet integration\n\n- mlx-vlm 0.5.0 is the floor for MTP support (`mlx_vlm.speculative.drafters.load_drafter`, `stream_generate(draft_kind=\"mtp\", draft_block_size=N)`).\n- `load_drafter()` returns `(model, resolved_kind)` — *not* a bare model.\n- Production `~/.gftd/venv` was **not** modified; bench used a dedicated `/tmp/mtp-bench/.venv` (uv, Python 3.13).\n- Drafter exposes `accept_lens: list[int]` after each generation — the only way to read acceptance counters from outside the streamer.\n- For T=0 the output is byte-identical to baseline (verified incidentally — same `text_head` across all configs per prompt).\n\n## Recommendation\n\n**Do not roll MTP out to the murakumo fleet for batch=1 inference on 4-bit targets.** The 1.11× best case doesn't justify the ~160 MB drafter footprint and added moving parts on the L8 Somatic tier (which is itself secondary to RunPod per ADR-2605080600).\n\nIf MTP is to be evaluated for production, the meaningful next experiments are:\n1. **bf16 target × batch 4–8** (mlx-vlm server) — the regime Google actually measured.\n2. **4-bit target with a 4-bit drafter** if/when one is published — closer logit alignment may lift acceptance enough to flip b=4 positive.\n3. Server-side draft-batching across concurrent streams (mlx-vlm doesn't currently support this).\n\nNone of (1)–(3) are committed work — listed only so the next person doesn't repeat this PoC's setup.\n\n## Reproduce\n\n```bash\n# from /tmp/mtp-bench (dedicated venv: mlx-vlm 0.5.0, Python 3.13)\nsource .venv/bin/activate\npython -u bench.py --warmup 1 --block-sizes 0,3,4,6,8 \\\n  --out /tmp/mtp-bench/results-full.json\n```\n\nHarness source: `/tmp/mtp-bench/bench.py`. Raw JSON: `/tmp/mtp-bench/results-full.json`.\n"}]